{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/1511097-force-and-mass-combat","id":1511097,"title":"Force and Mass Combat","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-08T19:31:39-07:00","id":1511097,"title":"Force and Mass Combat","content":"I am back from my break, and with it, I wanted to do a shorter homebrew to be used in my Essence document that would still work with the Core ruleset. I was inspired by the Force rules shown starting on pg. 119 to create a small expansion on what it can do. I didn't want to heavily alter what was already a simple rule set, so all I am doing here is providing some optional rules as well as a Storyguide parameter to use when creating enemies for this type of combat. Please let me know what you think below, and let's get started!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Judging a Rook By Its Cover: Approximating Force</span><br /> <br /> For Storyguides who want to best approximate the sort of advantage a given military force has, the following is recommended. Determining the base amount of Force a given group has depends not just on their numbers, but also their firepower, equipment and other things which can be approximated with Scale. To begin settling on a good metric, we have to accept a number of facts.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>The higher a given side's Force rating is, the likelier they are to win the conflict. This is the most basic rule of mass combat, and the one that must always be accounted for.</li> <li>Force is a metric which goes up or down depending on the situation and goal. This means that creative player decisions can, and should, allow them to alter the tides of a fight to favor their side.</li> <li>Groups of units are not all-around fantastic fighters, they have strengths and weaknesses. This means that they excel in certain situations, and fall short in others.</li> <li>Powered entities like Psions and Novas, as well as vehicles such as tanks and boats, are stronger and better than the average normal human. This means that a side which possesses these individuals or objects will likely beat those who don't, if there is no creative problem-solving involved.</li> </ol>With these points established, here is the way I've found to best approximate Force.<br /> <br /> First, when creating the group, determine the base unit which makes up that group. This will become our &quot;Base Force,&quot; which is the minimum amount that Force can go down to without extreme changes such as large amounts of death. For baseline characters, this is (Relevant Scale -1): The average human, by themselves, can do nothing against a group of fighters. If they had a grenade launcher, and the soldiers did not, they might have better odds. For powered individuals, this is (Higher of Tier or (Relevant Scale) - 1)). Psions and Talents have enough powers and luck on their side to allow them to fight half a dozen normal soldiers on their own, but anything more than this will prove a struggle. Novas and Proxies, even the ones most ill-suited to combat, are still generally smart or charismatic enough to work out ways to take/talk down about a dozen fighters by themselves.<br /> <br /> Vehicles instead rely on their large Scale to compensate for lack of powered abilities, assuming they possess none. This translates to them having a Base Force of (Higher of (Relevant Scale - 1) or (Size Scale - 2)). A truck even without guns can typically take on a few people before being stopped, simply through high-speed ramming. A battleship has so much armor that it can take on guns and missiles with relative ease.<br /> <br /> Once Base Force is established, the next point to consider is Scale. The most generally useful Scale for a given situation is Power or Speed, but a large enough Size Scale can also help boost these factors. To determine a group's total Size Scale, use their base amount and then add the following based on how many units there are:<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"width: 500px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Increase in Size Scale</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Number of units in group</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">Increase in Force</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+0</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+0</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">25</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+2</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">150</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+2</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">5,000</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+3</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">5,000,000</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+3</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">500,000,000</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+4</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">8,000,000,000</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+4</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">100,000,000,000</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 1px\">+5</td> </tr> </table></div> If a normal human has Size Scale 1, then 25 humans would constitute a Size Scale of 4. If a car has a Size Scale of 2, then 10 cars would have Size Scale 4, which would equal the prior group of humans. This <i>increase</i> in Size Scale is halved, and then added to the Base Force to determine the group's standard Force rating. So the 25 humans would have a Force rating of 2, while the 10 cars would have a Force rating of 3 ((Speed Scale 3 - 1 = 2, 2 + 1 from Size Scale Increase equals 3). This means that it would be a relatively equal fight between the two, but the cars would have an advantage.<br /> <br /> This Force Rating can change depending on what needs to be accomplished. A Formula One car might be very fast, helping it escape bad situations, but it probably doesn't have a lot of weapons to attack with. Always use the Relevant Scale for the stated goal of a given side.<br /> <br /> Other factors can affect Force, such as terrain, weather and motivation. Terrain can either provide a bonus or penalty of 1-3 for Force. Alternatively, it can create a Complication of the same amount for a given side (enemies if positive, yourself if negative). Weather as well as gravity conditions can provide a bonus or penalty of 1-2, or a Complication of the same, again depending on the situation. Motivation either changes Force by +/-1, or increases Difficulty by the same amount. Finally, other factors not named here can provide a bonus or penalty as the Storyguide sees fit. Once all of these factors are taken into account, you can now begin rolling each side's Force rating to see who wins.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Drama and Prolonging the Fight</span><br /> <br /> Generally, if one side reaches the Victory Threshold, that wins the fight for them. For the most important conflicts of a story, however, the Storyguide can set up multiple stages, or use either of the following two options to give their players (or if they're really mean, their enemies) another fighting chance: Cover and Last-Ditch Efforts. Both of these options should only be used if both sides are allowed to use them, it's not fair to give your bad guys a second wind if the player characters don't get the same.<br /> <br /> Cover despite the name does not just refer to stuff you can hide behind to avoid being shot. It also represents other things which provide an advantage to a given side which can be destroyed or consumed. Perhaps the people you are chasing manage to avoid your attempts to close in through a highway exit, but now they have to avoid slower traffic. Perhaps your characters denied the enemy their attempts to gain ground by large amounts of explosives, but now those are used up.<br /> <br /> When a given side uses Cover to prolong their fighting chance, they are reducing an advantageous factor <i>in their control</i> (so no using weather conditions this way unless you can control it via powers) to reset progress for the other side's victory. Subtract the Cover-using side's Force from the would-be victor's Force and note the remainder (minimum 1). Subtract this remainder from the bonus or penalty provided by the factor. This will almost always increase the victor's Force, reduce the loser's Force, or reduce a Complication the victor would face. In exchange however, the victor's progress towards the Victory Threshold is reset to 0, while the losing side keeps their progress.<br /> <br /> Let's say we have a group of soldiers with a normal Force rating of 3 hiding behind a brick wall, which provides +1 Force for a total of 4. They are facing a tank, which has an anti-tank gun, giving it a Power Scale of 4 and thus a Force rating of 3. The tank manages to roll 5 successes over the course of 2 turns, while the soldiers unfortunately only roll 1. Normally, this means that the soldiers are defeated, but their player decides that instead, the wall they are hiding behind is destroyed. This removes their advantage, putting them down to Force 3, but they still keep their lives and their 1 current success. Next turn, they roll 3 successes while the tank only rolls 1, enough to destroy it while only losing a few men... unless the tank is doing a Last-Ditch effort.<br /> <br /> Last-Ditch Efforts can only be done once per fight, and can only be performed if a given group's Scale for the situation is higher than 1. Like Cover, they subtract the loser's Force from the victor's, and then subtract the remainder (minimum 1) from EVERY category of Scale they have, to a minimum of 1. Armor is pierced, people can't think clearly in the thick of it, their guns jam, etc. The tank survives, but it's anti-tank gun is crippled, forcing it to use its lesser anti-infantry machine guns to fight back. This reduces its Power Scale to 3, and thus its Force to 2. At this point, it might have to keep fighting, because its Speed Scale (normally 2, now 1) is too awful by this point to be worth the effort.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26317-critian-caceorte","name":"Critian Caceorte","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}